\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
<% TRANSFORMATION TverbRaising
< m1:CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                [ mu2,
                  head/I1::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu7],
                  cliticrel/CLITIC{CLITICrec2},
                  complrel/I2::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                       [ mu3,
                         predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec2}
                             [ I4:mu9,
                               cliticrel/CLITIC{CLITICrec1}[I6: mu8],
                               I5:mu4
                             ],
                         mu10
                       ],
                  mu5
                ],
             mu6
           ]
>

< m :CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                [ mu2,
                  head/I3::VERB{VERBrec3}
                         [head/I1::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu7],
                          I4:mu9
                         ],
                  cliticrel/CLITIC{CLITICrec1}[I6: mu8],
                  complrel/I2::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                       [ mu3,
                         predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[I5:mu4],
                         mu10
                       ],
                  mu5
                ],
             mu6
           ]
>


   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       m:   CLAUSErec1.finiteness = finite
       m1:  CLAUSErec1.finiteness = finite
       I1:  VERBrec1.Clustering IN [ OptVR,OblVR ]
       I2:  ( SENTENCErec1.finiteness = infinite )     AND
                                (* V=Infinitivo or Gerundio *)
            ( SENTENCErec1.VRcompl = true ) AND
            ( SENTENCErec1.restructuring = false )
       I3:  VERBrec3.status = VV
       I4:  ALL (mu9, I41:rel3/..)
       I41: rel3 IN [auxrel, head]
       I5:  ALL (mu4, I51:rel2/..)
       I51: rel2 IN [vpadvrel, objrel, indobjrel]
       I6:  EXIST (mu8, [I61: rel4/..])
       I61: rel4 IN [unoclrel, dosclrel, tresclrel, seclrel]
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1:  VERBrec3 := VERBrec1;
               VERBrec3.status := VV;
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec3.status = VV
          A1: VERBrec1 := VERBrec3;
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TverbRaising
\item[* relevant examples:] puede [hacer-lo] $\rightarrow$ [puede hacer] lo [ ]
\item[* language:] Spanish
\item[* kind of rule:] M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] TC:SentenceOkrules
\item[* date of creation:] 20-JUN-1988 11:30:00  
\item[* author:] Elly van Munster
\item[* copied from:] --
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 09/11/88\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo
\item date: 14/11/88\ \ \ \ \ author: Elly van Munster\\
Condition "SENTENCErec1.VRcompl = true" added.
\item date: 09/01/89\ \ \ \ \ author: Lisette Appelo
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To form Verb raising structures.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:] To delete the VERBP in this rule as well.
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{All verbs except for {\em haber} are 
noclustering or optVR. {\em Haber} gets a special treatment.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Since at this moment only subject-control verbs allow Clustering, i.e. 
only verbs without there own (clitic)arguments,
the rule can be easy: the CLITICnode in the complrel just
moves to the right of VERBcluster-node under the CLAUSE.
The CLITIC-node of the nain clause (which must be empty) is deleted.\\
In the future, if also verbs with there own arguments allow Clustering, the 
rule will have to be more complicated, since the two CLITICnodes will have to 
merge. E.g.: \\
Me permite hacerlo $\rightarrow$ Me lo permite hacer.\\
If the verb is a subject-controller, the subject of main- and embedded sentence
is identical per definition, so the rule doesn't have to test this.\\
Again, if also (ind)object-controllers are added as verbraiser, the rule has to 
test if in these cases the (ind)obj of the main sentence is identical to the 
subject of the embedded sentence. \\
This rule is for finite clauses. If it should also be for infinite clauses, 
there are two options: 
1) The glue rules are sensitive for (very deep) embedded infinitives in verb 
clusters
2) This rule is extended with the possibility to put the glue + clitic into the 
verb cluster.\\
It is assumed that verbs in spansih are never oblVR except for {\em haber}. If 
such verbs would exist, this rule should allow for the absence of a CLITIC node 
in the embedded sentence. Now, there is always the path of noclustering and 
without clitics it is not possible to see if the verb is raised or not.\\
This rule supposes that the clitic in the embedded snetence is to the right of 
all the verbs. An exception to this is {\em haberlo hecho}. Then the clitic is 
between the auxiliary verb and the main verb. We need an extra rule for this 
possibility as the model is different.\\

\item modifications:
9/11/88: added glue to input model model;this is necessary as in the cycle of 
the sentence such a glue is put obligatorily between infinitives or gerundios 
and the clitics. 
Added mu8, mu9 and mu10: mu8, as clitic in sentence should not be empty (the 
one in clause is empty!), mu9, as at that place it is possible to have more 
than one verb and mu10, because it is possible to have adverbials to the right 
of the VERBP.
Added condition for finite clause. A rule for infinite clause has to be added 
or this rule has to be extended. It is still the question whether that rule is 
necessary.\\
9/1/89: Added SENTENCErec1.restructuring = false\\
\end{enumerate}
\end{description}


\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TPrepVerbRaising
< m1:CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                [ mu2,
                  head/I1::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu8],
                  cliticrel/CLITIC{CLITICrec2},
                  prepobjrel/PREPP{PREPPrec1}
                      [ head/PREP{PREPrec1},
                        objrel/I2::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                            [ mu3,
                              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec2}
                                  [ I4:rel1/T1,
                                    cliticrel/CLITIC{CLITICrec1},
                                    mu4
                                  ],
                              mu5
                            ]
                      ],
                  mu6
                ],
             mu7
           ]
>

< m :CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                [ mu2,
                  head/I3::VERB{VERBrec3}
                         [head/I1::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu8],
                          rel3/PREP{PREPrec1},
                          I4:rel1/T1
                         ],
                  cliticrel/CLITIC{CLITICrec1}[mu8],
                  complrel/I2::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                       [ mu3,
                         predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[mu4],
                         mu5
                       ],
                  mu6
                ],
             mu7
           ]
>


   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1:  VERBrec1.Clustering IN  [ OptVR, OblVR ]
       I2:  ( SENTENCErec1.finiteness = infinite )  AND
            ( SENTENCErec1.VRcompl = true ) AND
            ( SENTENCErec1.restructuring = true)
       I3:  VERBrec3.status = VV
       I4:  rel1 IN [head, auxrel]
       I5:  ALL (mu4, I51:rel2/..)
       I51: rel2 IN [vpadvrel, objrel, indobjrel]
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1:  @
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1:  @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TPrepVerbRaising
\item[* relevant examples:] empieza [a hacer-lo] $\rightarrow$ 
empieza a hacer-lo [ ]
\item[* language:] Spanish
\item[* kind of rule:] M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] TC:SentenceOkrules
\item[* date of creation:] 01-JUL-1988 11:30:00  
\item[* author:] Elly van Munster
\item[* copied from:] (TverbRaising)
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 14/11/88\ \ \ \ \ author: Elly van Munster\\
Condition "SENTENCErec1.VRcompl = true" added.
\item date: 09/01/89\ \ \ \ \ author: LIsette Appelo\\
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To form Verb raising structures for raising-verbs with a 
prepobjrel/SENTENCE (synPREPOPENINFSENT).\\
NB. In the literature it is often suggested that in these cases the "PREP"
is not really a PREP but some sort of complementizer, since it is assumed
that in a VCluster-node no material (other than a complementizer) can intervene 
between the two clustered verbs. In Rosetta no difference is made between a 
"normal" PREP and the PREP occurring with Verbraisers (e.g. empezar {\em a}).
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:] To delete the VERBP in this rule as well.
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Since at this moment only subject-control verbs allow Clustering, i.e. 
only verbs without there own (clitic)arguments,
the rule can be easy: the CLITICnode in the complrel just
moves to the right of VERBcluster-node under the CLAUSE.
The CLITIC-node of the nain clause (which must be empty) is deleted.\\
In the future, if also verbs with there own arguments allow Clustering, the 
rule will have to be more complicated, since the two CLITICnodes will have to 
merge. E.g.: \\
Me permite hacerlo $\rightarrow$ Me lo permite hacer.\\
If the verb is a subject-controller, the subject of main- and embedded sentence
is identical per definition, so the rule doesn't have to test this.\\
Again, if also (ind)object-controllers are added as verbraiser, the rule has to 
test if in these cases the (ind)obj of the main sentence is identical to the 
subject of the embedded sentence. 
\item modifications:\\
9/1/89: Condition "SENTENCErec1.restructuring = false" added.

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TRestructuring1  
< m1:CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                [ mu2,
                  head/I1::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu13],
                  cliticrel/CLITIC{}[mu7],
                  complrel/I2::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}
                       [subjrel/I10::NP{NPrec1}[mu15],
                        predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec2}
                              [head/VERB{VERBrec4}[mu11],
                               mu4
                              ],
                         mu10
                       ],
                  mu12
                ],
             mu3
           ]
>

< m :CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                [ mu2,
                  head/I3::VERB{VERBrec3}
                         [head/I1::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu13],
                          head/VERB{VERBrec4}[mu11]
                         ],
                  cliticrel/CLITIC{}[mu8],
