\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TRestructuring1
\item[* relevant examples:] 
Juan hace [venir Pedro] $\rightarrow$ Juan hace venir a Pedro\\
\item[* language:] Spanish 
\item[* kind of rule:]  M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] TC_SentenceOK
\item[* date of creation:] 08-AUGUSTUS-1991 14:47:12  
\item[* author:] Elena Pinillos
\item[* copied from:]
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to reduce complex structures with a causative main verb into
simpele structures; if the subordinate verb is an intransitive, the subject 
will demote into the DO position, getting Accustive case and clitic Doubling 
will not appear. If the subject is a pronoun, clitic rules will  in order to
 allow the clitic in the subordinate to climb 
into a position on the left of the causative main verb.
